# Line endings for the files whose bytes are their identity.
#
# A benchmark profile's bytes *are* its identity: every result records
# `benchmark.profile_sha256`, the SHA-256 of the profile file as it sits in the
# checkout that produced the run. With no rule here, git's autocrlf converts
# these files to CRLF on a Windows checkout, so the same profile hashes
# differently depending on which operating system a contributor cloned on --
# and git reports the working tree as clean the whole time, because it compares
# normalized content. `chat-small-extended-v1.yaml` was already in that state.
#
# The committed bytes are LF; this keeps the working tree matching them
# everywhere, so one profile has one hash.
benchmarks/profiles/*.yaml text eol=lf
catalog/**/*.json          text eol=lf
catalog/*.json             text eol=lf
schema/*.json              text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are run by bash under WSL/Linux. CRLF makes them fail with
# errors that name the interpreter rather than the problem.
*.sh text eol=lf
